      "id": 4,
      "kind": "mother_usage",
      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"preparation\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Research a general comparison-sort replacement for the per-bucket insertion sorts: sort record indices with an iterative mergesort or equivalent using the same bytewise string comparison as `strcmp`. This removes the quadratic behavior without depending on input distribution. Preserve the input records for output and compare full-program correctness and timing against baseline.\",\"B\":\"Research an independent LSD radix-sort candidate over normalized input tokens. Sort by bytes so the result matches the baseline’s string-comparison order; normalize shorter tokens with a terminating NUL and retain original tokens for output. Do not hard-code character frequencies or particular values. Measure the complete program, including parsing and output.\",\"C\":\"Research a parallel comparison-sort candidate for the ARM64 four-core target: sort bounded chunks of indices, then merge them with the same `strcmp` ordering, with a serial fallback for small inputs. Cap parallelism at the available cores and measure one- versus multi-thread performance; avoid races and unbounded scratch storage.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only candidates produced by A, B, and C, and preserve the best verified full-program result. Run correctness and timing checks on each candidate and each proposed combination: gains may not be additive, and integration can be slower than its inputs. Keep comparison/order semantics and behavior correct for every valid input; do not tailor to test data.\",\"analysis\":\"The task is bucketsort. In the supplied baseline source, each bucket uses insertion sort over indices with `strcmp`, giving a likely quadratic cost within large buckets; fixed per-bucket storage also merits care if bucket sizes are uneven. The only automatic measurement is a correct baseline run across 4 cases: mean 0.42005 s (samples 0.41999, 0.41831, 0.42185) and score 0.5. There are no measured optimized candidates, so these are opportunities to investigate, not established speedups. The target is ARM64 with 4 CPU cores.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"fbc518012bc19ae55d41f0304bea85f4fb80fc02d90c417cbd3160cead11620e\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Research a general comparison-sort replacement for the per-bucket insertion sorts: sort record indices with an iterative mergesort or equivalent using the same bytewise string comparison as `strcmp`. This removes the quadratic behavior without depending on input distribution. Preserve the input records for output and compare full-program correctness and timing against baseline.\",\"B\":\"Research an independent LSD radix-sort candidate over normalized input tokens. Sort by bytes so the result matches the baseline’s string-comparison order; normalize shorter tokens with a terminating NUL and retain original tokens for output. Do not hard-code character frequencies or particular values. Measure the complete program, including parsing and output.\",\"C\":\"Research a parallel comparison-sort candidate for the ARM64 four-core target: sort bounded chunks of indices, then merge them with the same `strcmp` ordering, with a serial fallback for small inputs. Cap parallelism at the available cores and measure one- versus multi-thread performance; avoid races and unbounded scratch storage.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only candidates produced by A, B, and C, and preserve the best verified full-program result. Run correctness and timing checks on each candidate and each proposed combination: gains may not be additive, and integration can be slower than its inputs. Keep comparison/order semantics and behavior correct for every valid input; do not tailor to test data.\",\"analysis\":\"The task is bucketsort. In the supplied baseline source, each bucket uses insertion sort over indices with `strcmp`, giving a likely quadratic cost within large buckets; fixed per-bucket storage also merits care if bucket sizes are uneven. The only automatic measurement is a correct baseline run across 4 cases: mean 0.42005 s (samples 0.41999, 0.41831, 0.42185) and score 0.5. There are no measured optimized candidates, so these are opportunities to investigate, not established speedups. The target is ARM64 with 4 CPU cores.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"nonce\":\"55478bbac4d04ef4b0e6281fdbbf4966\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"xhigh\",\"request_sha256\":\"fbc518012bc19ae55d41f0304bea85f4fb80fc02d90c417cbd3160cead11620e\",\"session_id\":\"fb704484f3a5459ca3a4a358d2208a0b\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"165061a90346dfe55aca6fee392433d96c9d105b6e852c2661628e94be7bc711\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"cached_input_tokens\":4224,\"input_tokens\":10829,\"output_tokens\":5281,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":4877}],\"llm_calls\":1,\"model_requested\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"reasoning_effort_requested\":\"xhigh\",\"token_accounting\":\"exact\",\"tokens\":16110,\"tool_events\":[]}},\"status\":\"complete\"}",
