    {
      "id": 17,
      "kind": "mother_usage",
      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"synthesis\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Research exact-size bucket ranges with a safe per-bucket sorting strategy. The stable mergesort candidate measured 14.302 ms, slower than the bucketed-qsort candidates, so compare it against qsort on the same inputs, including duplicate-heavy and skewed buckets. Preserve the source’s strcmp ordering and test boundary characters and tokens that share prefixes.\",\"B\":\"Continue evaluating radix sorting as an alternative, not as an assumed improvement: the cached-digit LSD variant measured 13.482 ms versus 14.437 ms for the other radix variant, but both trail the fastest bucketed-qsort measurements. Explore a bucket-local radix approach only if it preserves strcmp order for every valid token length; test ties and prefix cases, and avoid unsafe parallel histogram updates.\",\"C\":\"Refine the sharded-histogram, prefix-range, parallel-scatter, parallel-qsort design, which measured fastest at 13.029 ms. Audit and fix the shared outer `i` used by inner loops in the shown parallel regions; give each worker a private iteration variable, then remeasure. Use the 4-core target to investigate thresholds and scheduling under skewed bucket sizes. The serial-scatter/parallel-qsort candidate at 13.154 ms is a useful comparison.\",\"G\":\"Integrate conservatively: use the 13.154 ms serial-scatter/parallel-qsort candidate as a race-free reference, and consider the 13.029 ms sharded candidate only after correcting its shared-loop-variable race and remeasuring. Verify output correctness across valid inputs, especially bucket boundaries, skew, duplicates, and prefix-related tokens. Measure each integrated program as a whole; do not assume gains from combining the radix, mergesort, and parallel-bucket approaches will add.\",\"analysis\":\"This is Problem D: the source stores fixed-capacity string records, groups by first character, sorts each group with strcmp, then prints the strings. The baseline is correct on its reported cases and averages 0.42005 s; admissible variants measure 0.01303–0.01444 s, roughly 29–32× faster by those means. The fastest is the sharded C candidate, but its shown parallel loops share `i`, so its timing is not sufficient evidence of a safe implementation. The next-fastest C candidate uses serial counting/scattering and parallel qsort. Candidate correctness details are not included in the measurements, so verify correctness rather than infer it from speed.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"a0ae9ec38f9d7c73c5749a170bf13844b9a4e05bf112e30c4da9381f0c8ff3e2\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Research exact-size bucket ranges with a safe per-bucket sorting strategy. The stable mergesort candidate measured 14.302 ms, slower than the bucketed-qsort candidates, so compare it against qsort on the same inputs, including duplicate-heavy and skewed buckets. Preserve the source’s strcmp ordering and test boundary characters and tokens that share prefixes.\",\"B\":\"Continue evaluating radix sorting as an alternative, not as an assumed improvement: the cached-digit LSD variant measured 13.482 ms versus 14.437 ms for the other radix variant, but both trail the fastest bucketed-qsort measurements. Explore a bucket-local radix approach only if it preserves strcmp order for every valid token length; test ties and prefix cases, and avoid unsafe parallel histogram updates.\",\"C\":\"Refine the sharded-histogram, prefix-range, parallel-scatter, parallel-qsort design, which measured fastest at 13.029 ms. Audit and fix the shared outer `i` used by inner loops in the shown parallel regions; give each worker a private iteration variable, then remeasure. Use the 4-core target to investigate thresholds and scheduling under skewed bucket sizes. The serial-scatter/parallel-qsort candidate at 13.154 ms is a useful comparison.\",\"G\":\"Integrate conservatively: use the 13.154 ms serial-scatter/parallel-qsort candidate as a race-free reference, and consider the 13.029 ms sharded candidate only after correcting its shared-loop-variable race and remeasuring. Verify output correctness across valid inputs, especially bucket boundaries, skew, duplicates, and prefix-related tokens. Measure each integrated program as a whole; do not assume gains from combining the radix, mergesort, and parallel-bucket approaches will add.\",\"analysis\":\"This is Problem D: the source stores fixed-capacity string records, groups by first character, sorts each group with strcmp, then prints the strings. The baseline is correct on its reported cases and averages 0.42005 s; admissible variants measure 0.01303–0.01444 s, roughly 29–32× faster by those means. The fastest is the sharded C candidate, but its shown parallel loops share `i`, so its timing is not sufficient evidence of a safe implementation. The next-fastest C candidate uses serial counting/scattering and parallel qsort. Candidate correctness details are not included in the measurements, so verify correctness rather than infer it from speed.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"nonce\":\"1c554820ef2446f1b13e9a48f56679f1\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"xhigh\",\"request_sha256\":\"a0ae9ec38f9d7c73c5749a170bf13844b9a4e05bf112e30c4da9381f0c8ff3e2\",\"session_id\":\"fb704484f3a5459ca3a4a358d2208a0b\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"d27e3cac8bccd259d592188dd40f9f73a1d50a20c37d72c21fb38d081cd551bd\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"cached_input_tokens\":0,\"input_tokens\":23599,\"output_tokens\":3217,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":2707}],\"llm_calls\":1,\"model_requested\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"reasoning_effort_requested\":\"xhigh\",\"token_accounting\":\"exact\",\"tokens\":26816,\"tool_events\":[]}},\"status\":\"complete\"}",
      "created": 1792219280.1255276
